First, let's look at the last three digits of each term:
- For \(9 = 10^1 - 1\), the last three digits are 009.
- For \(99 = 10^2 - 1\), the last three digits are 999.
- For \(999 = 10^3 - 1\), the last three digits are 999.
- And so on, until \(10^{999} - 1\), which has the last three digits as 999.

So, the product modulo 1000 can be simplified to finding the last three digits of \(009 \times 999 \times 999 \times \cdots \times 999\) (with 998 factors of 999). We need to compute this product modulo 1000.

Notice that \(999 \equiv -1 \pmod{1000}\), so the product becomes:
\[009 \times (-1)^{998} \times (-1)^{998} \times \cdots \times (-1)^{998} \pmod{1000},\]
where there are 998 factors of \((-1)^{998}\) (which is 1) and one factor of 009. Therefore, the product simplifies to:
\[009 \times 1 \times 1 \times \cdots \times 1 \equiv 009 \pmod{1000}.\]

Thus, the remainder when \(9 \times 99 \times 999 \times \cdots \times \underbrace{99\cdots9}_{\text{999 9's}}\) is divided by 1000 is \(\boxed{009}\).
